1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:09,053
The brush clears a carved lotus, framed by a stone slab. In the early centuries, the Buddha was not shown as a figure.

2
00:00:09,053 --> 00:00:15,871
Artists used symbols. Later, two schools of sculpture took shape, Gandhara and Mathura.

3
00:00:15,871 --> 00:00:19,171
How can stone tell a story without a figure?

4
00:00:36,850 --> 00:00:45,886
How do we know? Archaeologists dug up the monasteries of Taxila and Mathura. Donors' names are carved on the bases of statues.

5
00:00:45,886 --> 00:00:49,354
The sculptures themselves are the main evidence.

6
00:01:05,517 --> 00:01:12,655
Early Buddhist art used symbols. Footprints, a wheel, an empty throne and a tree stood for the Buddha.

7
00:01:12,655 --> 00:01:21,414
Around the first century CE, in the Kushana age, images began to appear. There are three main schools of sculpture of this time.

8
00:01:21,414 --> 00:01:29,340
The Gandhara school grew in the north-west. It drew on Greek and Roman ways of carving, such as wavy hair and folded robes.

9
00:01:29,340 --> 00:01:39,091
It used grey schist stone. The Mathura school grew near the Yamuna. It used local red sandstone, and its work was Indian in feel.

10
00:01:39,091 --> 00:01:47,690
Mathura made images for Buddhists, Jains and Hindus. The Amaravati school, in the Krishna valley, used white marble.

11
00:01:47,690 --> 00:01:55,209
It carved long, flowing stories on stupa railings. Each school left its own mark on the art that followed.

12
00:02:11,383 --> 00:02:15,930
Scholars disagree. Where did the first images of the Buddha come from?

13
00:02:15,930 --> 00:02:20,848
One view says they began in Gandhara, under Greek and Roman influence.

14
00:02:20,848 --> 00:02:26,329
Another view says they began in Mathura, from an Indian tradition of carving figures.

15
00:02:26,329 --> 00:02:30,684
Some scholars think that both centres worked at about the same time.

16
00:02:46,850 --> 00:02:58,693
Carve this tip into memory. Gandhara: grey, Greek style. Mathura: red, Indian style. Grey and Greek, red and Indian.

17
00:02:58,693 --> 00:03:03,036
Add Amaravati's white marble, and you have three schools.

18
00:03:19,217 --> 00:03:35,092
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Which school used grey schist stone? The answer: Gandhara. Number two.

19
00:03:35,092 --> 00:03:52,312
Which school used red sandstone? The answer: Mathura. Number three. Which school carved on white marble in the Krishna valley?

20
00:03:52,312 --> 00:04:01,570
The answer: Amaravati. Now one line for Mains. Compare the Gandhara and Mathura schools of art.

21
00:04:21,750 --> 00:04:29,748
The lotus goes into its case. It is a symbol that stood for the Buddha before there were images. Three facts to take away.

22
00:04:29,748 --> 00:04:41,693
A lotus, an early Buddhist symbol. Gandhara, Mathura, Amaravati. Three schools, three stones. Next gallery: Trade With Rome.

